<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thurisind</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 560</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thurisind was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> from about 548 to about 560. His reign was dominated by war with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> under Audoin, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> supported each side in turn; his son Turismod was killed by Audoin's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> in battle around 551. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunimund">Cunimund</a>, the last Gepid king.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known of Thurisind's early life. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, an East Germanic people, held the eastern part of the Pannonian Basin, and their kingdom, Gepidia, had its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava River</a>, today Sremska Mitrovica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Around 548, after the death of King Elemund, Thurisind seized the throne in a coup d'état and forced Elemund's son Ostrogotha into exile among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. The scholar István Boná sees his rise as typical of the struggles among leading families that made the Gepid succession unstable. Thurisind made his eldest son Turismod commander at Sirmium, and so heir apparent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Emperor Justinian resented the Gepid takeover of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in 537 and had encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> under Audoin to move into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> to contain them. When Thurisind and Audoin both sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Justinian sided with the Lombards. In the first war, dated to 547 or 549, Thurisind offered a truce before a Byzantine army of 10,000 horsemen under John, magister militum of Illyricum, arrived. In 549 or 550 both armies panicked and no battle was fought, perhaps, Boná suggests, because of a lunar eclipse. Thurisind then ferried Kutrigur and Sclaveni raiders across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into Byzantine territory.</p><p>In 551 or 552 Audoin crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> at the Battle of Asfeld west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> counted among the bloodiest fought in the region, with no fewer than 60,000 killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a> writes that the duel in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> killed Turismod decided the war. Justinian imposed an eternal peace that preserved the Gepid kingdom, and Thurisind had to return <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> ripensis and Singidunum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>To settle the peace, Thurisind and Audoin each secretly had his royal guest murdered, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> pretender Ildigis and Ostrogotha. Paul the Deacon tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> came with 40 companions to Thurisind's court to be armed by a foreign king, and how Thurisind kept the peace and gave him Turismod's arms; the tale probably comes from a heroic poem. Thurisind died around 560. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunimund">Cunimund</a> was killed by Alboin in 567, when the Lombards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> destroyed the Gepid kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thurisind” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings <b>Thurisind</b> and Cunimund fought the Lombards in the 550s and 560s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-gepids">Kingdom of the Gepids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Pannonia, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=550" aria-label="Pannonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5003 578 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Thurisind"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5453 848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5453 848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=550">What was here in 550 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
